Heavy Periods: Causes, Symptoms and When to See a Doctor

Periods are different for everyone. Some people have light bleeding, while others experience heavier menstrual flow. However, when bleeding becomes unusually heavy, lasts longer than expected, or interferes with everyday activities, it may be a sign that you should speak with a healthcare professional.

What Are Heavy Periods?

Heavy menstrual bleeding refers to menstrual bleeding that is excessive enough to affect your normal routine or quality of life.

You may notice that you need to change your sanitary pad very frequently, experience repeated leakage, wake up during the night because of bleeding, or find that your period lasts much longer than usual.

One heavy day does not necessarily mean there is a medical problem. However, repeated heavy bleeding deserves attention.

Common Causes of Heavy Periods

There are several possible reasons for heavy menstrual bleeding.

Hormonal changes can affect how the uterine lining develops and sheds. This can sometimes result in heavier or longer periods.

Uterine fibroids and polyps are another possible cause. These growths can affect the uterus and may contribute to changes in menstrual bleeding.

Conditions such as adenomyosis and endometriosis may also be associated with heavy or painful periods. In some cases, thyroid problems, certain medications, or bleeding disorders can affect menstrual flow.

For teenagers, menstrual cycles may be irregular or unpredictable during the first few years after periods begin because the reproductive hormones are still developing.

Symptoms to Watch For

Signs that your period may be unusually heavy can include:

Frequently soaking through a sanitary pad
Repeated leakage onto clothes or bedding
Passing unusually large blood clots
Periods that consistently last longer than expected
Feeling extremely tired during or after your period
Dizziness or weakness
Shortness of breath during normal activities

Heavy menstrual bleeding can sometimes contribute to low iron levels. If you regularly feel unusually tired or weak, discuss your symptoms with a healthcare professional.

Choosing the Right Pad for Heavy Flow

While a sanitary pad cannot treat the cause of heavy bleeding, choosing an appropriate product can make daily activities more comfortable.

Longer pads, wider backs, and higher absorbency can provide additional protection. Overnight pads can also be useful when you want more front-to-back coverage while sleeping.

