Track Your Menstrual Flow

Keeping a simple period diary can be useful. Record the date your period starts, how many days bleeding continues, how frequently you change your pad, and whether you experience clots or significant pain.

This information can help you understand your normal pattern and can also be useful if you later discuss your symptoms with a doctor.

When Should You See a Doctor?

You should consider medical advice if your periods repeatedly become very heavy, last unusually long, occur between periods, or are associated with severe pain, dizziness, fainting, or significant weakness.

Do not assume that extremely heavy bleeding is something you simply have to tolerate. There may be an underlying reason, and a healthcare professional can help identify it.

Managing heavy periods involves more than choosing an absorbent sanitary product. Understanding your symptoms, tracking your cycle, maintaining good nutrition, and seeking medical advice when necessary are all important parts of menstrual health.
